Yes. “Consciousness” is probably not one scalar that is simply on or off. It is more useful to model it as a multidimensional state space.

Different dimensions can vary somewhat independently:

* Arousal — how awake/activated the system is.
* Phenomenal richness — how much sensory or experiential detail is present.
* Access — what information can enter reasoning, speech, and deliberate action.
* Attention — what receives processing priority.
* Self-modeling — how strongly experience is organized around “me.”
* Metacognition — awareness of your own thoughts, confidence, uncertainty, and errors.
* Interoception — awareness of internal bodily state.
* Agency — the felt distinction between causing something and merely observing it.
* Temporal depth — how much past and anticipated future are integrated into the present.
* Social consciousness — modeling other minds, intentions, relationships, and shared attention.
* Spatial/embodied consciousness — where the self and body seem located and bounded.
* Narrative consciousness — constructing continuity, identity, causes, characters, and meaning.
* Affective consciousness — valence, emotion, motivation, stakes.
* Conceptual consciousness — abstraction, categorization, symbolic thought.
* Counterfactual depth — representing what could have happened or could happen.
* Global integration — how widely information from different subsystems becomes mutually available.

So two people can both be fully awake while occupying radically different consciousness configurations.

Someone in flow may have:

\text{attention}\uparrow,\quad
\text{agency}\uparrow,\quad
\text{self-narration}\downarrow

Meditation can produce another configuration:

\text{interoception}\uparrow,\quad
\text{metacognition}\uparrow,\quad
\text{attachment to inner speech}\downarrow

Dreaming can have:

\text{imagery}\uparrow,\quad
\text{external sensory access}\downarrow,\quad
\text{reality monitoring}\downarrow

Severe pain can narrow the state:

\text{salience}_{pain}\uparrow
\Rightarrow
\text{temporal horizon}\downarrow,
\text{attention breadth}\downarrow

That is why describing consciousness with a single number like “level of consciousness” throws away enormous amounts of structure.

There are also different objects of consciousness:

\text{world}
,\;
\text{body}
,\;
\text{thought}
,\;
\text{self}
,\;
\text{other minds}
,\;
\text{relationships}
,\;
\text{time}
,\;
\text{possibilities}

And then there are different ways of being conscious of them: sensing something, knowing it, feeling it, attending to it, explicitly thinking about it, narrating it, or knowing that you know it.

That gives a useful tensor-like picture:

C =
C(
\text{content},
\text{modality},
\text{attention},
\text{access},
\text{selfhood},
\text{time},
\text{affect},
\text{agency},
\text{certainty},
\text{integration}
)

Which leads to an interesting implication: changing consciousness doesn’t necessarily mean gaining or losing “more consciousness.” Sometimes it means redistributing consciousness across dimensions.

You aren’t turning the light brighter.

You’re changing which rooms are illuminated, how strongly, and which doors between them are open.